<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beylerbey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as beylerbeys</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A beylerbey, meaning &quot;bey of beys&quot;, was the governor of a major province in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Beylerbeys commanded the provincial cavalry and oversaw the sanjak governors beneath them. The office was a central element of Ottoman provincial administration and military organisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beylerbey.jpg" alt="Beylerbey" width="320" height="525" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claes Rålamb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ralamb_-_103_-_Beylerbey_of_Bosnia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Beylerbey, from the Turkic for 'bey of beys', also rendered 'commander of commanders' or governor-general, was a high rank in the western Islamic world in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period. It began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, who used it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> for the army's commander-in-chief, and it was also used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, possibly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its Arabic equivalent was amir al-umara and its Persian equivalent mir-i miran.</p><p>In the early Ottoman state the beylerbey was a commander-in-chief held by princes of the dynasty: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a>, then Alaeddin Pasha and Süleyman Pasha. The change towards a governorship began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> gave the title to Lala Shahin Pasha for capturing Adrianople, with military authority over the Ottoman lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> (Rumelia). In 1393 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> named Kara Timurtash beylerbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and the beylerbeyliks of Rumelia and Anatolia, soon joined by Rûm, were the first and most important.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A beylerbey ran a province, termed a beylerbeylik or vilayet, and after 1591 an eyalet. Provinces were divided into sanjaks under sanjakbeys. As the empire grew in the 15th and 16th centuries the number of beylerbeys rose to a peak of 44 by the end of the 16th century; a list of 1609 names 32 eyalets. Their size varied from two sanjaks to twenty. The beylerbey of Rumelia ranked first and, after 1536, held a seat in the Imperial Council.</p><p>Within his province the beylerbey acted as a virtual viceroy, with authority over war, justice and administration. It was limited by officials appointed from the centre, chiefly the fiscal secretaries and the kadı, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> contingents stood outside his command. He kept his own court and divan and could grant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziamet">ziamets</a>, though after 1530 only the smaller timars. His chief duty was to maintain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahi</a> cavalry and lead it on campaign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> onwards beylerbey was also an honorary court rank, below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>; both were pashas, the viziers with three horse-tails and the beylerbeys with two. In the 18th century the Arabic title wali increasingly replaced beylerbey for governors-general, except in Rumelia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and the old titles became honorifics. The vilayet reform of 1864 made wali the only official title, and beylerbey survived only as the honorary rank Rumeli beylerbeysi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beylerbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It had specialised departments for the treasury, correspondence and provincial affairs, and a hierarchy of governors (<b>beylerbeys</b> and sancakbeys) whose authority reached from the Crimea to Yemen and from Algeria to the Persian Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Until 1587 it was governed by <b>beylerbeys</b>, many of them leading corsairs such as Hayreddin’s son Hasan Pasha and Uluj Ali.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
